  "summary": "The LAPD let its Flock ALPR contract expire after an Inspector General audit found that in a two-month period officers investigated 161 people whose vehicles were incorrectly flagged as stolen, often due to outdated hot list data from other jurisdictions. The audit found 210.5 million plate reads generated 5,911 tracked plates with no action on 4,575, while yielding 337 stolen car recoveries and 74 arrests. LAPD said it would not enter new ALPR contracts pending a full audit.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "During the review period, officers acknowledged 161 alerts as accurate license plate matches; however, subsequent investigations determined the vehicles were not stolen",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "The LAPD allowed its Flock contract to expire over the weekend, and said it would not enter into new contracts until going through a full audit process.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "suspend the deployment of new ALPR cameras and the execution of new ALPR-related contracts pending public input and a broader reassessment of vendors and data practices",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
